"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_mathematics_en"][8]={"school_mathematics_en:M8-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M8-L1 – Measurement scales, regression and correlation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Advanced data analysis<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education · elective\nextension<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L8<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Placement:<\/strong> Elective extension under the Bavarian\nLehrplanPLUS M12; not compulsory nationwide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>You distinguish measurement scales, calculate a least-squares line\nand Pearson’s correlation coefficient, interpret residuals and identify\nlimitations of statistical models. You compare grouped and pooled data\nand explain a reversal of direction in Simpson’s paradox.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement scales and meaningful analyses<\/h2>\n<p>The meaning of a number depends on how it is measured. A nominal\nvariable distinguishes categories without a ranking (e.g. bus or\nbicycle); numeric codes do not create measurable distances. Frequencies\nand the mode are meaningful. Ordinal variables have a ranking (e.g. low,\nmedium, high) but not necessarily equal intervals; medians and rank\ncomparisons are possible. On an interval scale, differences are\ninterpretable, as with Celsius temperatures, but ratios of readings are\nnot. A ratio scale also has a meaningful zero, as with duration or\nlength. Two hours are twice one hour; 20 °C is not twice as warm as 10\n°C.<\/p>\n<p>Discrete counts can also be quantitative. The key question is whether\nan operation has a meaningful interpretation. For the following linear\nregression, we use two quantitative variables with meaningful\ndifferences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scatterplots and least squares<\/h2>\n<p>Each data point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_i,y_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a pair of measurements from the same observation. We select\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the explanatory variable and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the response. A line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=a+bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides fitted values; residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i=y_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare vertical deviations. Least squares minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(a,b)=\\sum_i e_i^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot geometric distances to the line.<\/p>\n<p>Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xx}=\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{yy}=\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xy}=\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xx}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}},\\qquad a=\\bar y-b\\bar x.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Why? For fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe quadratic function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis minimised at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\bar y-b\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution leaves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=S_{yy}-2bS_{xy}+b^2 S_{xx}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompleting the square yields the stated minimum. The line therefore\npasses through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\bar x,\\bar y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare equal, the slope is not uniquely determined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and correlation<\/h2>\n<p>An artificial dataset contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2),(2,4),(3,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe means are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xx}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{yy}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xy}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=2+0.5x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe fitted values 2.5, 3, 3.5 yield residuals −0.5, 1, −0.5. Their sum\nis zero and their sum of squares is 1.5.<\/p>\n<p>For two nonconstant quantitative variables,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}},\\qquad -1\\le r\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts sign agrees with the slope’s sign. Its magnitude describes the\nstrength of the linear association, not the magnitude of the slope. For\nsimple regression with an intercept,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=r^2=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe residual sum of squares is 25% smaller than for a constant-mean\nmodel. This does not mean that 25% of observations were predicted\ncorrectly. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis undefined. Positive rescaling of units leaves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunchanged but generally changes the slope.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simpson’s paradox: consider groups<\/h2>\n<p>Consider two artificial groups A with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,6),(2,7),(3,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand B with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,1),(6,2),(7,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithin each group\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter pooling, the means are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=4.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xx}=28<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>26<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xy}=-26<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe pooled slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.929<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-13/14\\approx-0.929<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe direction reverses because the groups differ in both their typical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlevels.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_043_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Group A is upper left and group B lower right. Both within-group lines increase, but the pooled regression line decreases.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Two increasing groups and a decreasing pooled\nline.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>This illustrates Simpson’s paradox. Pooling is not an arithmetic\nerror; it answers a different question from a within-group comparison.\nThe relevant grouping must be determined from the data collection\nprocess and research question. Statistical grouping alone cannot\nestablish causality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and checks<\/h2>\n<p>Correlation can result from a shared influencing variable. Even\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not establish causality. Conversely, a strong nonlinear\nrelationship can yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1,0,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis zero despite complete functional dependence. Outliers can strongly\nchange the line and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthey must not be removed without a substantive justification.<\/p>\n<p>First draw a scatterplot, calculate the line, plot residuals against\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand look for patterns. Check units, missing values, grouping and\nprovenance. Predictions outside the observed range are extrapolations\nand require additional assumptions. Even inside the range, a line is not\na guarantee for individual observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Classify mode of transport, rank, Celsius temperature and journey\nduration using the four measurement scales.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the line and correlation for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1),(2,3),(3,5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the example in S4, calculate the fitted value at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand explain why this is interpolation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the groups in S5, determine both individual lines and the\nintercept of the pooled line.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use the example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom S6 to explain why\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not imply independence.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S7\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=-1+2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nall residuals zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=3.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe value 2.5 is between 1 and 3, the observed range.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=x+5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nB:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=x-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPooled:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>115<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.214<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=4.5+52/14=115/14\\approx8.214<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the three symmetric values, the positive and negative products\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{xy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel, giving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNevertheless,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis uniquely determined by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-business-statistics-2e/pages/1-3-levels-of-measurement\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Business Statistics 2e, 1.3 Levels of Measurement<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\n1.3 · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/mathematics/linear-regression-and-linear-relations/correlation-and-least-squares-regression-line\">Monash\nUniversity – Correlation and least squares regression line<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Pearson\ncoefficient, least squares, interpolation · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://rafalab.dfci.harvard.edu/dsbook/association-is-not-causation.html\">Rafael\nA. Irizarry – Introduction to Data Science, 19.5 Simpson’s paradox<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\n19.5 · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_mathematics_en:M8-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M8-L2 – Independence testing and critical data analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Advanced data analysis<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary education · elective\nextension<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L16&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L16<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Placement:<\/strong> Elective extension under the Bavarian\nLehrplanPLUS M12; not compulsory nationwide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>You construct contingency tables, calculate expected counts under\nindependence and perform Pearson’s chi-square test. You distinguish\nstatistical significance from effect size and causality and examine\nmedia claims for distorted denominators or unjustified conclusions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Contingency tables and hypotheses<\/h2>\n<p>We consider two categorical variables. In a hypothetical random\nsample of 100 independently selected people, we record preferred\nlearning format (video or text) and preferred study time (morning or\nevening). Each person belongs to exactly one cell. The observed counts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Format<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Morning<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Evening<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Total<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Video<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The null hypothesis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstates that learning format and study time are independent in the\nunderlying population. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor every combination of categories. The alternative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstates that this fails for at least one combination. A significance\nlevel, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis chosen before analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Expected counts and test statistic<\/h2>\n<p>Under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe estimate marginal probabilities using observed marginal proportions.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobservations,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{ij}=n\\cdot\\frac{R_i}{n}\\cdot\\frac{C_j}{n}=\\frac{R_iC_j}{n},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the row total and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe column total. Expected counts need not be integers. In the example\nall four expected counts are 25. Expected marginal totals agree with\nobserved marginal totals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-mathematics-en/media/img/S_MAT_044_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Observed table 30, 20, 20, 30; expected counts under independence are all 25. Each cell contributes 1 to the test statistic of 4.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Comparison of observed and expected\ncounts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Pearson’s test statistic is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/munderover><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>m<\/mi><\/munderover><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>O<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sum_{j=1}^{m}\\frac{(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With four cells having deviations of ±5,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2=4\\cdot25/25=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLarger values indicate greater discrepancies relative to expected\ncounts, but do not alone determine a decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Degrees of freedom and p-value<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns, there are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k-1)(m-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndegrees of freedom: when marginal totals are fixed, the upper-left\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k-1)\\times(m-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nblock can be chosen freely within the count constraints; the remaining\ncells are determined. Under appropriate conditions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas approximately a chi-square distribution under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith these degrees of freedom. We use the uncorrected Pearson\nstatistic.<\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">df=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe p-value is the probability under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof obtaining a test statistic at least as large:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0455<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=P(\\chi^2_1\\ge4)\\approx0.0455<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe reject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe do not. The p-value is neither the probability that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis true nor the probability that the result is merely due to chance.\nFailure to reject does not establish independence.<\/p>\n<p>The test requires independent observations and mutually exclusive\ncategories. For school-level use we adopt the conservative rule of thumb\nthat every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{ij}\\ge5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEmpty marginal categories must be excluded beforehand; for small\nexpected counts, the approximation can be unsuitable (for a 2×2 table,\nFisher’s exact test is one alternative). Repeated responses from one\nindividual are not independent observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible software analysis and effect size<\/h2>\n<p>With Python and SciPy installed, the computation can be\nreproduced:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> chi2_contingency<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>observed <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x2, p, df, expected <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> chi2_contingency(observed, correction<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(x2, p, df, expected)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The expected output is 4, approximately 0.0455, 1, and a 2×2 matrix\ncontaining four 25s. The option <code>correction=False<\/code> matters\nbecause software may otherwise apply a continuity correction for one\ndegree of freedom. Alternatively, calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a spreadsheet and obtain the right-tail probability using its\nchi-square function. Record software, data, settings and rounding.<\/p>\n<p>A descriptive effect size for 2×2 tables is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\phi|=\\sqrt{X^2/n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhere it is 0.2. The observed morning proportions are 60% (video) and 40%\n(text), a difference of 20 percentage points. Doubling each cell\npreserves that difference and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\phi|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut doubles\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto 8; the p-value drops to approximately 0.00468. This compares two\nhypothetical samples; it is not a justification for duplicating\nobservations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Critical analysis of media claims<\/h2>\n<p>The claim “video learning causes a preference for mornings” does not\nfollow from the table. An unmeasured timetable could influence both\nvariables. Before interpreting results, check sample selection, missing\nresponses, exact group definitions and the period of data collection.\nCompare proportions with their proper denominators, and distinguish\nabsolute counts, percentages and percentage points. A bar chart with a\ntruncated axis can make small differences look large. Inspect axes and\nunits; display uncertainty when available.<\/p>\n<p>Testing many associations after observing data increases the chance\nof at least one apparently notable result. Report nonsignificant results\nas well, label exploratory analysis and distinguish it from prespecified\nhypotheses. Significance alone establishes neither relevance nor\ncausality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate all expected counts for the table\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>20<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>10<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>10<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>20<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}20&amp;10\\\\10&amp;20\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">df<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the decision at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor exercise 1. Use the critical value 3.841.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 3×4 table has positive marginal totals and sufficiently large\nexpected counts. How many degrees of freedom does the test have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a table with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2=3.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\phi|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif it is a 2×2 table. Can a causal claim be made?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An article reports 20 of 50 versus 30 of 100 as “50% more success”\nfor the second group. Assess the claim and name one further piece of\ninformation needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S7\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Row and column totals are all 30;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEvery\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{ij}=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^2=4\\cdot25/15=20/3\\approx6.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">df=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.667<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>3.841<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.667&gt;3.841<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(subject to the test assumptions).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3-1)(4-1)=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\phi|=\\sqrt{3.2/80}=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNo, association does not establish a cause.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absolute counts rise from 20 to 30, but proportions fall from 40% to\n30% (−10 percentage points). Relevant information includes sample\nselection, time period or definition of success.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/11-3-test-of-independence\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 11.3 Test of Independence<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 11.3 ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
